                fiki-go reads the target URL and RFC 8941 as sent, beyond what the KERI vectors pin = decision:
                  nid: 8f6txftu
                  why: >-
                    Behaviour fiki-go adopts in reaching parity that no vector in vectors/ or vectors/keri/ pins, recorded because a change nothing tests is a change nobody can see. URL AS SENT: the port splits the target URL itself, as RFC 3986's generic syntax and Python's urlsplit do, rather than through net/url, because url.Parse decodes the path and @path must be the path as sent, percent-encoding included and unnormalized (profile section 2, O1); the old port read url.URL.Path and so signed a decoded path. Leading C0 controls and spaces are stripped and tab, CR and LF removed, as the WHATWG parser and urlsplit both do, a fragment is dropped, and a URL is never refused for its shape. Only @authority can fail: a port that is not a decimal from 0 to 65535 is a MissingComponent naming @authority, where fiki-py raises a bare ValueError from outside its taxonomy; failing closed with a kind is the port's choice and is not a vectors matter. IPV6: @authority keeps the brackets of an IP literal, [::1]:8443, because RFC 3986 section 3.2.2 makes them part of the host and RFC 9421 section 2.2.3 builds @authority from it; fiki-py drops them through urlsplit's hostname, which is a fiki-py bug ticked separately and is deliberately not copied, so fiki-py and fiki-go build different bases for an IPv6 target until it is fixed. The js port made the same choice on 2026-10-07. RFC 8941: the parser now reads tokens and decimals, caps an integer at 15 digits and a decimal at 12 integer and 3 fractional digits as RFC 8941 sections 3.3.1 and 3.3.2 require, overwrites a duplicated parameter in place as section 4.2.3.2 requires, and refuses a string byte outside visible ASCII as section 3.3.3 requires. Tokens and decimals are read so that a header carrying one is refused for what it means, an unsupported component parameter or a wrongly typed signature parameter, rather than as unparsable, which is the refusal fiki-py gives through http_sfv. Rejected keeping net/url and reconstructing the raw path from url.URL.RawPath, which Go only populates when its own re-encoding differs and silently normalizes otherwise. Tradeoff: a hand-rolled URL splitter is one more parser to keep right, bounded by the request vectors that carry encoded paths, a colon in a path and a re-encoded tilde.
                fiki-go refuses small-order Ed25519 keys as MalformedKey = decision:
                  nid: 8krqtpsu
                  why: >-
                    fiki-go refuses an Ed25519 public key of small order, the identity point and the other seven points whose order divides 8, in any encoding canonical or not, as MalformedKey, wherever the key comes from: a raw keyid, an ExpectedAID, or a Resolver's answer, and always before the signature is checked. This answers tick 27eo, which the conductor relayed as a requirement for every port on 2026-10-07. The reason is a forgery, not a style point: Go's crypto/ed25519, like fiki-py's OpenSSL, accepts the signature 0x01 followed by 63 zero bytes under the key 0x01 followed by 31 zero bytes over ANY message, because the verification equation [S]B = R + [k]A holds trivially when A is the identity and S is zero; the same family of shortcuts exists for every small-order A. A raw keyid is attacker-chosen, so without this check the identity point's keyid authenticates anything; a resolver is supposed to be authoritative, but a registry that can be made to hold such a key would be a forgery oracle. MalformedKey rather than SignatureMismatch, because the defect is in the key, it is found before any signature is examined, and the KERI profile's section 9 puts the key group ahead of the signature group. DETECTION without a dependency: the Edwards y coordinate is mapped to the Montgomery u = (1 + y) / (1 - y) mod 2^255 - 19 with math/big, y = 1 (the identity) caught directly, and crypto/ecdh's X25519 is used as the small-order test, since RFC 7748 scalars are clamped to multiples of 8 and Go returns an error exactly when the shared secret is all zeros, which for a clamped scalar happens exactly when the point's order divides 8; a y that names no curve point but lands on a small-order point of the quadratic twist is refused too, which is fail closed for a key that could never verify anyway. Chose that over a hard-coded blocklist of encodings, because AGENTS.md forbids byte-exact values taken from memory or a summary and a blocklist is nothing but byte-exact values, and over hand-written Edwards arithmetic, which is more code to get wrong than one field division. The tests derive the order-8 points independently, by solving d y^4 + 2 y^2 - 1 = 0 from the curve equation, so the check and its oracle do not share a method. Rejected refusing mixed-order keys (a large-order point plus a torsion component) too, which libsodium does not do and no attack here needs. Tradeoff: fiki-go now refuses a key fiki-py accepts until fiki-py is fixed, and no shared vector pins either behaviour until the next vectors_format bump adds one. EXTENDED 2026-10-07 by the conductor's ruling on tick 27eo: any 32-byte public key that is not the canonical encoding of a point on the curve is MalformedKey too, on the same paths and at the same point: a y with no x on the curve, a y at or above p even when it reduces to a valid large-order point, and the sign bit set where x is 0. Go's crypto/ed25519 already refuses such keys, but only by failing every signature, which reported them as SignatureMismatch, a forgery verdict for what is a defect in the key and one section 9 places after the key group. The check decodes y with math/big, refuses y >= p, solves x^2 = (y^2 - 1) / (d y^2 + 1) and refuses a non-square, and refuses the sign bit on x = 0; the tests classify y by Euler's criterion instead of a square root, so again the check and its oracle do not share a method.
        fiki-py decodes an RFC 8941 byte sequence the same way on every Python, with padding only at its end = decision:
          nid: 2g4xxev9
          why: >-
            fiki-py checks every byte sequence in the Signature, Signature-Input and Content-Digest headers itself, after http_sfv has parsed the header and before the value is used: its content must be base64 whose only '=' characters are the one or two that complete the final four-character quantum. Anything else -- data after the padding, an '=' in the middle, padding beyond what completes the quantum -- is refused as the header's own malformed class (MalformedSignature, MalformedSignatureInput, MalformedDigest). This answers tick 4joc, reproduced 2026-10-07 by decoding every string of up to nine characters over 'A', 'Q' and '=' on each interpreter: http_sfv decodes with Python's lenient base64.standard_b64decode, which stops at the first padding and ignores what follows through 3.12 and reads past it from 3.13, so 11,168 of those strings decode differently or fail on one side only. The split is 3.11-3.12 against 3.13-3.14, not 3.11-3.13 against 3.14 as the tick reported. In fiki's terms the same py release accepted a Signature header carrying the true signature followed by 'AAAA' on 3.11 and 3.12 and refused it on 3.13 and 3.14, and refused an '=' in the middle on 3.12 as MalformedSignatureValue while accepting it on 3.13. Grounds, read from the RFC text: RFC 8941 section 4.2.7 step 7 base64-decodes by RFC 4648, and says it does not relax RFC 4648 sections 3.1 and 3.3; section 3.3 makes the pad character before the end of the data, and excess pad characters at the end, non-alphabet data that an implementation MUST reject unless the referring specification says it may ignore them, and RFC 8941 does not say so. MISSING PADDING stays refused, on every interpreter as before. Section 4.2.7 says a parser SHOULD NOT fail when '=' padding is absent, unless it cannot be configured to accept it; http_sfv cannot, and accepting it would mean rewriting the header before handing it to the parser, a second parser in all but name. Recorded as a deviation from that SHOULD rather than fixed here. NON-ZERO PAD BITS stay accepted, as section 4.2.7 asks and as every interpreter already did. Rejected patching http_sfv's decoder, which would change it for every other user of http_sfv in the process, and rejected scanning the raw header before parsing it, which would put fiki's refusal ahead of the parser's and so change which class a doubly-malformed header gets. The scan runs on a header that has already parsed, where a colon opens a byte sequence only when it starts a bare item, never inside a string or a token. Tradeoff: excess padding ('...===') is newly refused on every interpreter, where all four accepted it; and a port that follows Python 3.14's decoder, as the C# port is reported to, still accepts an '=' in the middle that fiki-py now refuses, so the ports must converge on this rule and a vector should pin it at the next vectors_format bump.
        The C# port takes BouncyCastle for Ed25519, targets netstandard2.0 and net10.0, and ports fiki-py as it stands = decision:
          nid: 5l4p36rl
          why: >-
            The C# port lives in csharp/ and follows @2tt6fmc0's shape -- the same names in .NET casing, the error taxonomy pinned by the vectors, a required freshness choice -- with three differences, each Daniel's decision of 2026-10-07. FIRST, IT TAKES A CRYPTO DEPENDENCY, because .NET has no Ed25519 in its standard library: the API is approved (dotnet/runtime#63174) but has not shipped, and the .NET 11 release candidate adds X25519 key agreement, which is a different operation. So C# sits beside Rust in @2tt6fmc0's dependency table. Chose BouncyCastle.Cryptography over NSec.Cryptography because BouncyCastle is pure managed code: NSec wraps libsodium and ships native binaries per runtime identifier, and a native library that fails to load inside somebody's enterprise container is a failure fiki's audience (@07wstqk7: an ESB client, a cron job) would hit at the worst moment. BouncyCastle also derives the public key from a 32-byte seed directly, which spares this port the provider workaround Java needed. Rejected hand-written curve arithmetic, for the reason the Java README gives. Tradeoff: a large library of which fiki uses one algorithm, and one more maintainer outside Bakobo in the supply chain. The RFC 8941 subset stays hand-rolled, on @2q9gv70t's reasoning. SECOND, IT TARGETS netstandard2.0 AND net10.0. netstandard2.0 reaches .NET Framework 4.8, which is where many of the ESB clients @07wstqk7 names still run; net10.0 is the current LTS. Rejected net10.0 alone, which is simpler and would shut out .NET Framework entirely, and net8.0 as the floor, since its support ends in November 2026. Tradeoff: the netstandard2.0 build compiles against a smaller API, so the code is written to that floor, and CI must run the suite on .NET Framework itself (a Windows runner) for the claim to be checked rather than asserted. THIRD, THE REFERENCE IS fiki-py AS IT STANDS, NOT THE JAVA PORT. The js, go, java and rust ports still uppercase @method, which @22g0xkr8 reversed, and none of them implements the KERI profile (@8vwrexxc, @7f28p7xk, @6g9zjsv9). Porting from Java would start C# as a sixth port already behind; porting from py/src/fiki makes it the second port that passes both vectors/ and vectors/keri/. So the vector drivers assert both vectors_format and keri_vectors_format. Tradeoff: more work up front, roughly half again the size of the Java port, and the C# port will for a time be ahead of four of its siblings. Coverage is held at 100% branch through coverlet, which .NET can enforce where Java and Rust today cannot (tick 4buh).
    The key travels inline in keyid, as raw bytes rather than as the AID = decision:
      nid: 7xrx5evg
      why: >-
        The signer's key travels in RFC 9421's own keyid parameter, as the RAW 32-byte Ed25519 public key in base64url with no padding -- the RFC 8037 JWK "x" form, 43 characters -- and NOT as the 44-character B-prefix AID. So a request carries its own verifying key inline and a verifier resolves nothing; the AID remains what a human registers and reads, and fiki derives it from the keyid on the verify side rather than transmitting it. This is not a fresh choice: heti's vanilla dialect already fixes it at src/heti/ephemeral/vanilla.py:_resolve_key, whose @c3m5wp chose the inline raw-key form over a directory lookup because both of its recovery paths must stay pure, and cited draft-hardt-httpbis-signature-key and Cloudflare's web-bot-auth as the external convention. fiki emitting the AID there instead would mean heti could not verify a fiki-signed request at all, which would defeat the interop the split exists to preserve, so the decision is inherited rather than reopened. Chose one form rather than accepting both, because a keyid a verifier has to sniff is an interop ambiguity that shows up as a signature failure at somebody else's site. Chose to keep sign_request's keyid NON-overridable, unlike the lower-level signature_base, so that "the keyid is the key" is a property of every fiki-signed request rather than a default: the RFC's own Appendix B.2.6 vector uses a symbolic keyid ("test-key-ed25519") and is reproduced through signature_base for exactly that reason. Rejected carrying the AID in a separate header the way the KERI dialect carries Signify-Resource, which would put the identity outside RFC 9421's own vocabulary and give an off-the-shelf verifier one more thing it does not implement. Tradeoff: a verifier holding a preregistered AID must convert it to raw bytes to compare against keyid, which is two lines but is a conversion that would not exist if the AID travelled directly; and a fiki-signed request is pseudonymous by construction, since an inline key attests to itself and to nothing else.
      children:
        A caller may choose the keyid, and a caller-supplied resolver is authoritative for it = decision:
          nid: 6g9zjsv9
          why: >-
            sign_request and the new sign_response accept a caller-supplied keyid string, such as a KERI AID, and verify_request and verify_response accept a caller-supplied resolver, a callable from the keyid string to the 32 raw bytes of an Ed25519 public key. Without either, nothing changes: the keyid is still the inline raw key of @7xrx5evg and a verifier still decodes it. This revises one clause of @7xrx5evg, that sign_request's keyid is not overridable. The KERI profile makes keyid the signer's AID (profile R1), a transferable AID does not contain its current key, and a signify-ts or keripy verifier has to resolve it against a key event log, so fiki cannot produce the profile's vectors while "the keyid is the key" is a property of every fiki-signed request. Daniel decided on 2026-09-24 to keep fiki KERI-free and hand the resolution to the caller: fiki learns nothing about CESR beyond the non-transferable B-code lens it already has, and a caller who holds KEL state supplies the function that reads it. WHEN A RESOLVER IS SUPPLIED IT IS AUTHORITATIVE and fiki never falls back to decoding the keyid as a key. That is the safety property, not a detail: a basic transferable prefix D... embeds its inception key, and a verifier that decoded it would accept a signature from a key rotated away, undoing pre-rotation (profile R1, [SPC-F6]). A resolver that returns None means the keyid names no key the caller knows, which is UnknownKey, a new class; a resolver may itself raise MalformedKey for a keyid that is not a well-formed identifier, and fiki propagates it. In resolver mode the Verdict's aid is the keyid the resolver vouched for, since the key's own B rendering is not the signer's identity there. Supplying both expected_aid and a resolver is a programming error, refused with TypeError, because the two answer the same question and a silent precedence rule would be a trap. Rejected teaching fiki to decode transferable prefixes itself, which is the start of a CESR implementation and the start of keripy's dependency surface (@07wstqk7). Rejected making the keyid form a verifier sniffs from its first character, which the profile forbids outright because D... would sniff as a key. Tradeoff: a fiki-signed request with a caller-chosen keyid is no longer self-verifying, so the pseudonymous inline-key property of @7xrx5evg now holds only for the default; and resolver correctness is entirely the caller's, including rotation.
        fiki-py refuses a public key that is small-order or not a canonical on-curve encoding, as MalformedKey = decision:
          nid: 37wdchu5
          why: >-
            fiki-py refuses, as MalformedKey and before any signature is examined, an Ed25519 public key that is not the canonical encoding of a point on the curve -- y at or above p, a y with no x on the curve, or the sign bit set where x is 0 -- or that is a point of small order, the identity and the seven other points whose order divides 8. That holds wherever the key comes from: a raw keyid, an AID decoded by verifying_key (so expected_aid too), or a resolver's answer. This is tick 27eo and the conductor's decisions of 2026-10-07 (D-C3BH, widened by D-QMDN), which every port follows. The reason is a forgery: fiki-py, through cryptography and OpenSSL, accepted the signature 0x01 followed by 63 zero bytes under the key 0x01 followed by 31 zero bytes over ANY message, because [S]B = R + [k]A holds trivially when A is the identity and S is zero; a raw keyid is attacker-chosen, so that keyid authenticated anything, and a registry that could be made to hold such a key would make a resolver a forgery oracle. An off-curve key could never verify, but fiki-py reported it as SignatureMismatch, a forgery verdict for a defect in the key, which the KERI profile's section 9 orders after the key group. MalformedKey because the defect is the key's and is found first. DETECTION IN PURE PYTHON, with no new dependency (@07wstqk7 fixes fiki-py's dependencies at two): the key is decompressed exactly as RFC 8032 section 5.1.3 decodes a point, refusing what that procedure refuses, and the point is then doubled three times in extended coordinates (RFC 8032 section 5.1.4's doubling) and refused if the result is the identity, which is the definition of an order dividing 8. cryptography exposes neither test. Rejected a hard-coded blocklist of encodings in the libsodium style, because AGENTS.md forbids byte-exact values taken from memory and a blocklist is nothing but such values; the tests instead derive the eight small-order points from the curve equation (order-8 points satisfy d y^4 + 2 y^2 - 1 = 0) with a general square root, so the check and its oracle do not share a method, and cover all fourteen encodings libsodium's has_small_order names. Rejected refusing mixed-order keys, which libsodium does not do and no attack here needs. Tradeoff: about forty lines of field arithmetic in keys.py, run once per verification, and a key fiki-py used to accept now fails; no honest key can be small-order or off-curve, since a clamped secret scalar times the base point is neither. No shared vector pins this until the next vectors_format bump.
    'fiki reads a clock: expires is always enforced, max_age is a required decision = decision':
      nid: 67shl6c5
      why: >-
        fiki reads a clock, and verify_request enforces freshness. Two conditions, decided separately because they have different owners. EXPIRES IS ALWAYS ENFORCED and takes no parameter: the signer put it there, it is bound into the signature base so it cannot be tampered with, and RFC 9421 section 3.2.1 says a verifier may reject a signature ahead of its expiration but never that it may ignore one -- a library that accepts an expires it does not honor is selling the caller a guarantee it has not bought, which is the defect ~3qdj named. MAX AGE IS A REQUIRED KEYWORD with no default, so a caller states a tolerance in seconds or states None, and either way the decision is written at the call site. Chose a required argument over a default because both defaults are wrong: a value guesses at somebody else's clock skew and replay window, and None reproduces the silent skip this whole change exists to remove -- the same fail-closed reasoning @2hwvpm42 used for the body. Section 3.2.1 is the authority for both halves: it lists maximum-age enforcement and expiry rejection as APPLICATION requirements rather than baseline ones, and then says that when an application defines a requirement it MUST enforce it during verification. So the RFC does not tell fiki what the policy is; it tells fiki that a declared policy must bite. A skew allowance defaults to 5 seconds and is adjustable, because two hosts disagreeing by a second is ordinary and a verifier that treats that as an attack is unusable. `now` is injectable, which is what keeps @5gf6r08f's vectors byte-exact: a conformance vector cannot pin a check against a moving clock, and a port would otherwise have to fake time to run them. fiki reads the clock ONLY when it needs to -- when expires is present, or when max_age is not None -- so a request that declares no freshness at all still verifies deterministically. Rejected enforcing a default max_age: it would break every existing vector, all of which are signed at a fixed created in the past, and breaking them is the loud symptom of a guess nobody made deliberately. Rejected leaving expires to the same required-parameter treatment as max_age: the two are not symmetric, since expires is the SIGNER's declaration and max_age is the VERIFIER's, and requiring a verifier to opt in to honoring a signer's own expiry inverts who is asserting what. Tradeoff: verify_request is no longer a pure function, so fiki's purity claim now has an exception with a name; heti's vanilla dialect must pass max_age through and passes None explicitly, which is heti's standing position under @p3w5kq rather than an oversight, and a heti caller who wants freshness now has somewhere for that to go.
    '@method is the method as sent, with no case transformation = decision':
      nid: 22g0xkr8
      why: >-
        fiki stops uppercasing @method. The component value is the method exactly as the caller supplies it, because RFC 9421 section 2.2.1 defines @method as the method as sent, case-sensitive, with no transformation, and HTTP methods are case-sensitive tokens (RFC 9110 section 9.1): "post" and "POST" are different methods, and a verifier that folds them computes the same base for both. Daniel's decision of 2026-09-24, prompted by the KERI profile (@8vwrexxc), which pins a lowercase-method vector so every implementation behaves the same. The consequence the RFC anticipates is the caller's burden: browsers' fetch() uppercases the standard methods on the wire, so a signer has to be handed the method as it will actually be sent. Chose to fix fiki-py only in this change and file ticks for js, go, java and rust, all four of which uppercase today, because the shared vectors exercise only uppercase methods and are unaffected, so no port goes red and vectors_format does not move -- the fix is observable only with a lowercase method, which none of the shared vectors carries. That is also why it is not a format bump under @4fhrre0m: no shared vector's required behaviour changed. heti and heti-wallet consume fiki; they are affected only when a caller signs or verifies a lowercase method, which after this change must be sent in the case it was signed in. Rejected keeping the uppercase fold as a convenience: it is a normalization the RFC does not permit, and it makes a fiki verifier accept a request whose method differs from the one signed. Tradeoff: until the four ticks land, fiki-py and the other ports disagree on lowercase methods while all five declare vectors_format 1, which is exactly the drift @4fhrre0m's number exists to expose and here cannot, because no shared vector pins it.
